    <h1>Accessibility statement</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last reviewed: <time datetime="{{ site.privacyUpdated }}">21 November 2026</time></p>

    <p class="lead">We want everyone to be able to use the Dexteria website, including people who use a keyboard, a screen reader, magnification or reduced motion. We aim to meet the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 at level AA.</p>

    <h2>What you can do on this site</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use every link, button and form field with a keyboard alone.</li>
      <li>Skip straight to the main content with the first link on the page.</li>
      <li>Stop the scrolling slogan, floating shapes and the ball’s idle float with the <strong>Pause motion</strong> button at the top of the page.</li>
      <li>Turn on “reduce motion” in your device settings to remove scroll effects. The page then shows everything as a simple, still layout.</li>
      <li>Zoom text to 200% and use the site on screens as narrow as 320 pixels without scrolling sideways.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How we tested it</h2>
    <p>We checked the site with automated testing (axe), keyboard-only navigation, reduced-motion settings and screen widths of 320, 768 and 1440 pixels, and we checked every text colour against WCAG contrast requirements.</p>

    <h2>Known limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Some pictures are placeholders until the final photos and app screens are ready. Their descriptions explain what each one shows.</li>
      <li>The animated Smart Ball is decorative. It carries no information that is not also given in text.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tell us about a problem</h2>
    <p>If something on this site does not work for you, email <a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}">{{ site.contactEmail }}</a>. Tell us the page and what happened, and we will try to fix it.</p>

    <p><a href="/">Back to the Dexteria home page</a></p>
